// Wonder Lab ancient history data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py and tools/grow/build_course_js.py
// Years are never `found` — see check_history and .work/BRIEF-ancient.md.
// 78 facts across 13 sections.
const ANCIENT = [{"id":"beginning-1","section":"beginning","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis opens with six days of making and a seventh of rest. Light, sky, sea and land, plants, sun and moon and stars, fish and birds, land animals and people. It is written as a sequence of days, each one closing with an evening and a morning.","more":"Genesis is a written record. You can go and read it, which is a different kind of thing from a measurement or a calculation."},{"id":"beginning-2","section":"beginning","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 5 is a list. Ten men from Adam to Noah, and for each one it gives the age he was when his son was born and how long he lived after. Genesis 11 does the same thing again from Noah's son Shem down to Abraham.","more":"Lists like this are why a timeline is possible at all. Most ancient writing does not tell you how many years passed between one man and the next."},{"id":"beginning-3","section":"beginning","cat":"when","kind":"worked","text":"Add up the family lists in Genesis 5 and 11 and you get a number of years from creation to Abraham. An Irish archbishop named James Ussher did exactly that. He published his answer in 1650. Creation, he said, was the night before the 23rd of October, 4004 BC.","more":"That date is arithmetic, not a verse. Nowhere does the Bible say 4004. Ussher added the genealogies, then anchored the total to a known Babylonian king's death that could be tied to a dated eclipse.","tryit":"Add up your own family instead. How old was your mother when you were born? Her mother when she was born? Keep going back as far as anyone can remember and see how many years you get."},{"id":"beginning-4","section":"beginning","cat":"writing","kind":"worked","text":"Archbishop James Ussher's 4004 BC for creation is not the only total. The old copies of Genesis do not all carry the same numbers. The Greek Septuagint gives longer gaps in the same genealogies. Add those up and creation lands nearer 5500 BC, about 1,500 years earlier.","more":"Both are honest arithmetic on a written list. They differ because the lists differ. Anyone who gives you one number without mentioning that is leaving something out."},{"id":"beginning-5","section":"beginning","cat":"artifact","kind":"record","text":"The oldest complete Hebrew Bible that still exists is the Leningrad Codex, copied by hand in AD 1008. It is in the Russian National Library in St Petersburg. Older still is the Aleppo Codex, from around AD 930, but parts of it are missing.","more":"The scribe signed and dated the book at the back, which is how the year is known. Every printed Hebrew Bible today is set from this manuscript."},{"id":"beginning-6","section":"beginning","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The oldest known piece of the Bible is small enough to fit in your palm. Two rolled silver strips, found in a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem in 1979, carry the priestly blessing from Numbers 6 scratched into the metal.","more":"They were so fragile it took three years to work out how to unroll them without destroying them. They are in the Israel Museum."},{"id":"flood-1","section":"flood","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"Genesis gives the Ark's measurements. It was 300 cubits long, 50 wide and 30 high. A cubit is about 18 inches, so that works out at roughly 450 ft long, 75 ft wide and 45 ft high. That is longer than a football field's 100 yards.","more":"Those are the numbers in the text. The shape is not described beyond a door, a roof and three decks, so every picture you have ever seen of it involves somebody guessing.","tryit":"Take a tape measure outside and mark out 450 ft. Walk from one end to the other and count your steps. That is one boat."},{"id":"flood-2","section":"flood","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In the British Museum there is a broken lump of clay covered in wedge marks. It tells of a great flood, a boat, and birds sent out to find land. It was dug up at Nineveh and is called the Flood Tablet.","more":"It is part of a Babylonian poem called the Epic of Gilgamesh. The flood account on it is not the same as Genesis. The names are different, the gods are different, the boat is different. It is still unmistakably a story about the same kind of event."},{"id":"flood-3","section":"flood","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"George Smith worked at the British Museum, sorting tablets nobody had read. In 1872 he made out the flood lines on one of them. A colleague who was in the room says he became so excited that he jumped up and started taking his clothes off.","more":"He then traveled to Iraq to look for the missing piece of the tablet, and found it. That almost never happens."},{"id":"flood-4","section":"flood","cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Flood accounts turn up in the written traditions of peoples all over the world. Many of those peoples had no contact with each other. The list runs to Babylon, Greece, India and China. It takes in peoples across the Americas and the Pacific as well.","more":"What that means is argued about. Some say it points back to one real event remembered everywhere; others say big floods happen everywhere and so do stories about them. The written accounts themselves are simply there, in libraries, and you can read them."},{"id":"flood-5","section":"flood","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 11 says that at Babel the whole earth had one language, and that afterwards people could not understand one another and scattered. The tower is described as brick and tar, not stone.","more":"The detail about brick is the kind of thing that only matters if you know the region. Southern Iraq has almost no building stone. Everything there really was built of mud brick."},{"id":"flood-6","section":"flood","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"Languages sort into families. English, Greek, Hindi and Farsi share so many words and word-endings that they are grouped together as one family. So are Hebrew and Arabic, in a different one. Nobody has managed to link all the families to a single root.","more":"Families are worked out by comparing words across hundreds of languages and looking for patterns that hold every time. Latin pater and English father. Latin pes and English foot. Whether the families join up further back is not something anyone has shown."},{"id":"egypt-1","section":"egypt","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Great Pyramid's base covers 13 acres and its four sides differ in length by less than 8 inches. The ground it sits on was levelled to within about an inch across the whole thing. It was built 455 ft high and has lost about 26 ft off the top.","more":"Those are measurements anyone can go and check, and people have, repeatedly, with better instruments each time. The precision is the astonishing part, not the size."},{"id":"egypt-2","section":"egypt","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"You will often read that the Great Pyramid contains 2.3 million blocks. Nobody has counted them. The figure comes from measuring the outside, working out the volume, and dividing by the average size of the blocks you can see.","more":"It is a reasonable estimate. It might also be well out. Nobody knows how much of the inside is solid rock left in place rather than cut blocks."},{"id":"egypt-3","section":"egypt","cat":"when","kind":"worked","text":"Most books date the Great Pyramid to about 2560 BC. That number is built from Egyptian king-lists — copies made centuries later of records of who reigned and for how long — lined up with carbon dates from mortar. Nothing on the pyramid gives a year.","more":"Add up the genealogies in the Bible and that date falls within a few centuries after the Flood. Ussher's reckoning would not allow it that early at all. Both numbers are worked out. Neither one was read off the stone.","tryit":"Ask someone how they know when something old was made. If the answer is just \"they worked it out\", ask what they worked it out from. That is always the interesting part."},{"id":"egypt-4","section":"egypt","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In room 4 of the British Museum there is a slab of dark stone with the same decree cut into it three times, in three different scripts. French soldiers dug it out of a wall at Rashid in 1799. It is the Rosetta Stone.","more":"Because one of the three was Greek, which people could still read, it became the key to hieroglyphs. Jean-François Champollion announced he had cracked them in 1822."},{"id":"egypt-5","section":"egypt","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"Hieroglyphs are not picture-writing in the way people expect. Most of the signs stand for sounds, not things. A king's name is written inside an oval loop called a cartouche. That is how Jean-François Champollion got his first foothold in cracking them — he went looking for Ptolemy.","tryit":"Look up a hieroglyph alphabet chart and write your own name in a cartouche. The signs for sounds English does not have will make you improvise, which is exactly what the Egyptians did with foreign names."},{"id":"egypt-6","section":"egypt","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"To make a mummy, the body was packed in natron — a natural salt — for about forty days to dry it out. The lungs, liver, stomach and intestines were taken out and stored separately. The brain was removed through the nose with a hooked rod and thrown away.","more":"The heart was left in. They thought that was where a person did their thinking, so it had to stay. We know the method from Egyptian texts and from unwrapping mummies and looking."},{"id":"mesopot-1","section":"mesopot","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"The oldest writing we can still read was pressed into wet clay with the cut end of a reed. Each press leaves a wedge, which is why it is called cuneiform — Latin for wedge-shaped. Hundreds of thousands of these tablets have been dug up.","more":"Most have never been translated. There are more untranslated cuneiform tablets sitting in museum drawers than there are translated ones.","tryit":"Flatten a lump of plasticine and press a popsicle stick end into it at different angles. Try to make a mark you could tell apart from every other mark you have made. It is harder than it looks."},{"id":"mesopot-2","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In the Louvre stands a finger of black stone 7 ft 5 in tall with 282 laws cut around it — the Code of Hammurabi. It was found not in Babylon but at Susa in Iran, in 1901, because somebody had carried it off as loot.","more":"The laws cover farm rent, wages, surgery gone wrong, and what happens if a house falls down and kills someone. It is one of the oldest legal documents anybody can read."},{"id":"mesopot-3","section":"mesopot","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The ziggurat at Ur in southern Iraq is a stepped mud-brick platform with three great staircases meeting at the top. The lowest stage is still standing and has been rebuilt. People climb those stairs today.","more":"It is made of millions of mud bricks with a baked-brick skin held together with tar. Some of the bricks are stamped with a king's name, which is a bit like finding a builder's mark."},{"id":"mesopot-4","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Standard of Ur is a wooden box about 19 in long, covered in shell and blue lapis lazuli, showing rows of little figures. One side shows a feast. The other shows wagons — four solid wheels each, pulled by donkeys.","more":"It came out of a royal tomb dug by Leonard Woolley in the 1920s and is in the British Museum. Nobody actually knows what it was for."},{"id":"mesopot-5","section":"mesopot","cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"The earliest wheels that survive are not on carts. They are potter's wheels — flat discs spun to shape clay. Wheels for carrying things show up later, made of three planks pegged together into a solid disc, not spokes.","more":"A solid wooden wheel is enormously heavy. A spoked wheel is not. Spoked wheels came much later, and they changed everything."},{"id":"mesopot-6","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Archaeologists digging at Nineveh found the remains of a king's library. It is around 30,000 clay tablets and fragments, collected by a king called Ashurbanipal. There are letters, omens, medicine and word-lists. The poem carrying the flood account is in there too.","more":"The palace burned down. Fire destroys paper and parchment; it bakes clay harder. The library survived because it was destroyed."},{"id":"israel-1","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1993 a broken piece of black basalt turned up in a wall at Tel Dan in northern Israel. Cut into it, in Aramaic, is a boast by a king about defeating his enemies — and the phrase \"House of David\".","more":"It is the earliest mention of David found outside the Bible. Before it was dug up, some argued he was entirely legendary. The stone is in the Israel Museum in Jerusalem."},{"id":"israel-2","section":"israel","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Under Jerusalem there is a tunnel 1,750 ft long, cut through solid rock, that still carries water. It was dug to bring a spring inside the city walls before a siege. You can walk it today, in the water, with a flashlight.","more":"Two teams cut it, starting from opposite ends. The tunnel wanders in an S-shape, and the pick marks change direction where they met.","tryit":"Measure out 1,750 ft on a map of your own street. That is how far two teams cut through rock, in the dark, and still met."},{"id":"israel-3","section":"israel","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"In 1880 a boy wading in the water tunnel cut through rock under Jerusalem noticed writing on the wall. It describes the last moments of the dig. Two teams cutting from opposite ends call to each other through the rock. Then the pick-axes break through, and the water runs.","more":"The inscription was cut out of the wall and is now in the Archaeology Museum in Istanbul. It is one of very few ancient inscriptions written by the workmen rather than about a king."},{"id":"israel-4","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"An Assyrian king had a whole siege carved along the walls of his palace at Nineveh. It shows the town of Lachish, with siege ramps, archers, battering rams and the people leaving. The whole wall is now in the British Museum.","more":"Excavators later dug at Lachish itself and found the siege ramp, the burned layer, and heaps of sling stones and arrowheads. The carving and the ground agree with each other."},{"id":"israel-5","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1947 a shepherd threw a stone into a cave near the Dead Sea and heard pottery break. Inside were jars of scrolls. One is a complete book of Isaiah, 24 ft long, written on 17 sheets of leather sewn together.","more":"It is displayed in a building in Jerusalem shaped like the lid of the jars. What it showed is that the text had been copied for a very long time with remarkably few changes."},{"id":"israel-6","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Cyrus Cylinder is a barrel of clay about 9 in long, covered in cuneiform. It was dug up at Babylon in 1879. On it, King Cyrus says he sent captured peoples back to their own homes. He let them rebuild their temples.","more":"That is the same policy the book of Ezra describes from the other side. The cylinder does not mention Judah by name. What it shows is that Cyrus really did work this way."},{"id":"greece-1","section":"greece","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Parthenon has almost no straight lines in it. The floor rises about 4 inches in the middle of the long sides. The columns lean slightly inwards and bulge in the middle. Measure it and you find hardly anything is truly flat or upright.","more":"Why is argued about — a correction for how eyes work, or simply how it looked best. The measurements themselves are not in doubt; people have surveyed that building for two hundred years."},{"id":"greece-2","section":"greece","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Sponge divers working a shipwreck off Antikythera in 1901 brought up a corroded green lump. Inside it were bronze gears — at least thirty of them, cut with triangular teeth, meshed together.","more":"X-rays and scans have since read the inscriptions inside it. It tracked the sun and moon and predicted eclipses. Nothing else of that complexity survives from anywhere near its time."},{"id":"greece-3","section":"greece","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Archimedes worked out how levers multiply force and is quoted as saying that with a place to stand he could move the earth. He also designed a screw inside a tube that lifts water uphill when you turn it. Farmers still use that design.","tryit":"Balance a ruler on a pencil with a book on one end. Move the pencil closer to the book and press the other end. Notice how much less effort it takes the further away you push."},{"id":"greece-4","section":"greece","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"The Greeks borrowed their letters from the Phoenicians. Then they added something the Phoenicians never wrote down: vowels. Our A, B and E are those same letters, handed on through the Romans. A few thousand years of copying wore the shapes smooth.","more":"Alpha and beta are Greek letter names. Put them together and you have the word alphabet."},{"id":"greece-5","section":"greece","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Greeks kept written lists of who won at the Olympic Games, event by event. The prize was a wreath of wild olive. There was no second place recorded.","more":"Those winner-lists are useful far beyond sport. The games came round on a fixed cycle, so Greek writers dated other events by counting games. That gives historians a ruler to measure other records against."},{"id":"greece-6","section":"greece","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"A runner is supposed to have carried news from Marathon to Athens and then died. That story is not in the earliest account. Herodotus wrote closest to the event, and he describes a runner going the other way. That runner went to Sparta to ask for help, a much longer run.","more":"The dying-messenger version turns up centuries later. It might be true. But when the earliest writer tells a different story, that is worth knowing before you repeat the famous one."},{"id":"rome-1","section":"rome","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Roman concrete harbors are still standing in seawater, which modern concrete does not manage. The Romans mixed volcanic ash into theirs. Seawater reacts with that ash and grows new crystals inside the concrete, so it gets stronger instead of crumbling.","more":"This was worked out by drilling cores out of Roman harbors and looking at them under microscopes. The recipe was never lost, exactly — it was written down by Vitruvius. It just stopped being used."},{"id":"rome-2","section":"rome","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Pantheon in Rome is roofed by a concrete dome 142 ft across with a 27 ft hole open to the sky in the middle. There is no steel in it. It is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world, and the rain still comes in.","more":"The concrete gets lighter toward the top — heavy stone in the mix at the bottom, light volcanic rock near the opening. There are drains in the floor."},{"id":"rome-3","section":"rome","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"The aqueduct that fed the Roman town of Nîmes runs about 31 miles and drops only 41 ft over that whole distance. That is a slope of roughly one inch in every 250 ft. They surveyed it with water levels and sighting rods.","more":"Too steep and the water tears the channel apart; too flat and it stops. The Pont du Gard, the bridge that carries it across a valley, is 160 ft tall and still standing."},{"id":"rome-4","section":"rome","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"When Vesuvius buried Pompeii, bodies left holes in the hardened ash as they decayed. In 1863 an excavator started pouring plaster into the holes before digging them out. What comes out is the shape of a person in their last moment.","more":"Pliny the Younger watched the eruption from across the bay as a teenager and wrote two letters describing it. His account and the ash layers match."},{"id":"rome-5","section":"rome","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"The Colosseum held around 50,000 people and had 80 entrances. Spectators were given numbered tokens telling them which arch to use and where to sit. The numbers are still carved above the arches.","more":"It could be emptied fast, which is the hard part of any big building. Modern stadiums are laid out on the same principle."},{"id":"rome-6","section":"rome","cat":"writing","kind":"worked","text":"A surprising share of ordinary English words came from Latin by way of French. Animal, family, question, city, table, letter, number. The short blunt words are usually the older English ones: house, food, eat, work.","tryit":"Read a page of any book and try to sort the words into short blunt ones and long smooth ones. The long ones are mostly Latin arriving late."},{"id":"patriarchs-1","section":"patriarchs","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The written account says Yahweh told Abram to leave his country and his father's house. Abram was to go to a land that Yahweh would show him. Yahweh promised to make of him a great nation.","more":""},{"id":"patriarchs-2","section":"patriarchs","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The written account says Abram was very rich. He had livestock, silver and gold.","more":""},{"id":"patriarchs-3","section":"patriarchs","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Abram lived in the land of Canaan, and Lot lived in the cities of the plain. Lot moved his tent as far as Sodom.","more":""},{"id":"patriarchs-4","section":"patriarchs","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Abraham split the wood for the offering and went to the place God had told him about. On the third day, he looked up and saw the place far off.","more":""},{"id":"patriarchs-5","section":"patriarchs","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"God said that all the nations of the earth would be blessed through Abraham's offspring. Then Abraham returned to his young men, and they went together to Beersheba.","more":""},{"id":"patriarchs-6","section":"patriarchs","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The written account says Joseph was seventeen years old when he was feeding the flock with his brothers. Their father Israel loved Joseph more than all his children and made him a coat of many colors.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-1","section":"exodus","cat":"when","kind":"record","text":"The written account says Yahweh spoke to Moses and Aaron in Egypt. He said this month would be the first month of the year to them.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-2","section":"exodus","cat":"daily","kind":"record","text":"Each family's lamb had to be without defect, a male a year old. They kept it until the fourteenth day of the month.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-3","section":"exodus","cat":"daily","kind":"record","text":"Israel was told to take some of the lamb's blood and put it on the two door posts and the lintel of their houses.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-4","section":"exodus","cat":"daily","kind":"record","text":"Israel was to eat the Passover meal ready to go. They wore belts on their waists and shoes on their feet, with a staff in the hand. They ate it in haste.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-5","section":"exodus","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Moses stretched out his hand over the sea. Yahweh caused the sea to go back by a strong east wind all night. The waters were divided, and the sea became dry land.","more":""},{"id":"exodus-6","section":"exodus","cat":"food","kind":"record","text":"The people called the food manna. It was white like coriander seed, and it tasted like wafers with honey. They ate it for forty years, until they came to the borders of Canaan.","more":""},{"id":"kings-1","section":"kings","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"Goliath of Gath, the Philistine champion, was six cubits and a span tall. He wore a brass helmet and a coat of mail that weighed five thousand shekels.","more":""},{"id":"kings-2","section":"kings","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"David took his staff in his hand and chose five smooth stones out of the brook. He put them in the pouch of his shepherd's bag.","more":""},{"id":"kings-3","section":"kings","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"The house that King Solomon built for Yahweh was sixty cubits long, twenty cubits wide and thirty cubits high.","more":""},{"id":"kings-4","section":"kings","cat":"built","kind":"record","text":"King Solomon spent seven years building the house of Yahweh. In the end it was finished throughout all its parts, just as planned.","more":""},{"id":"kings-5","section":"kings","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The queen of Sheba heard about Solomon's fame and came to test him with hard questions. She came to Jerusalem with a very great caravan, with camels carrying spices, a lot of gold and precious stones.","more":""},{"id":"kings-6","section":"kings","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"Once every three years the fleet of Tarshish came to King Solomon. It brought gold, silver, ivory, apes and peacocks.","more":""},{"id":"babylon-1","section":"babylon","cat":"built","kind":"record","text":"Babylon was an ancient city on the Euphrates river in Mesopotamia. Its ruins are in Iraq, about 53 mi (85 km) south of Baghdad.","more":""},{"id":"babylon-2","section":"babylon","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"Babylon may have been the first city with more than 200,000 people. It covered an area of about 3.5 sq mi (900 ha).","more":""},{"id":"babylon-3","section":"babylon","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"The Ishtar Gate was 49 ft (15 m) high, and its foundations went another 46 ft (14 m) underground.","more":""},{"id":"babylon-4","section":"babylon","cat":"artifact","kind":"record","text":"The Processional Way ran through the Ishtar Gate. Its walls showed about 120 lions, bulls, dragons and flowers on yellow and black glazed bricks.","more":""},{"id":"babylon-5","section":"babylon","cat":"built","kind":"record","text":"The Hanging Gardens of Babylon were one of the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. They were described as rising tiers of gardens with many trees, shrubs and vines, like a large green mountain.","more":""},{"id":"babylon-6","section":"babylon","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The written account says Daniel was taken up out of the den of lions. No kind of harm was found on him, because he had trusted in his God.","more":""},{"id":"persia-1","section":"persia","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"At its peak, the Persian empire covered about 2.1 million sq mi (5.5 million square kilometers). It was the largest empire of its time.","more":""},{"id":"persia-2","section":"persia","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"Under Darius the Great, the empire stretched from Southeast Europe and Northeast Africa in the west to the Indus Valley in the east.","more":""},{"id":"persia-3","section":"persia","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Cyrus is honored in Judaism for freeing the Jewish people from captivity in Babylon. He did it by issuing the Edict of Restoration.","more":""},{"id":"persia-4","section":"persia","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"According to the Book of Isaiah, Yahweh anointed Cyrus for this task and called him a messiah. Cyrus is the only non-Jewish person honored in this way.","more":""},{"id":"persia-5","section":"persia","cat":"speed","kind":"record","text":"Mounted couriers were supposed to travel the 1,677 miles (2,699 km) from Susa to Sardis in nine days. On foot the trip took ninety days.","more":""},{"id":"persia-6","section":"persia","cat":"built","kind":"record","text":"The Royal Road was built for fast communication across the western part of the empire, from Susa to Sardis. It was probably perfected under Darius I.","more":""},{"id":"sea-1","section":"sea","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"The Phoenicians were an ancient people who lived in city-states along the eastern Mediterranean coast. Most of their land was in what is now Lebanon.","more":""},{"id":"sea-2","section":"sea","cat":"travel","kind":"record","text":"The Phoenicians traded all over the Mediterranean Sea. They went from Cyprus to the Iberian Peninsula. They left thousands of inscriptions.","more":""},{"id":"sea-3","section":"sea","cat":"home","kind":"found","text":"Tyre juts out from the coast of the Mediterranean Sea, about 50 mi (80 km) south of Beirut. It is one of the oldest cities in the world that people have lived in without a break.","more":""},{"id":"sea-4","section":"sea","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"The Phoenician alphabet was one of the first alphabets. It was written horizontally, from right to left, and was the first to have a fixed writing direction.","more":""},{"id":"sea-5","section":"sea","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"The Phoenician alphabet used one symbol for each sound. That meant there were only a few dozen symbols to learn.","more":""},{"id":"sea-6","section":"sea","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"The Phoenician alphabet used 22 letters, all of them consonants. It left the vowel sounds for the reader to supply.","more":""}];
globalThis.ANCIENT = ANCIENT;
